      "writtenOn": "2023-05-26T21:52:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What\u0027s the rationale for storing the offsets into the virtual address range of the regsiters? I understand that you save some time in computing this, but is it worth the cost in memory? Suppose you had a large register space? I think it might be best to compute these values on the fly?",
      "revId": "ba35458c874bc0349c36b805a10200648e4b6738",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4360b8c7_9e648e61",
        "filename": "drivers/cpufreq/kvm-cpufreq.c",
        "patchSetId": 21
      },
      "lineNbr": 151,
      "author": {
        "id": 1937676
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-06-03T21:03:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Since I\u0027d expect the cpufreq MMIO interface to be quite simple, I don\u0027t expect there to be a large cost in memory even if there were a large number of vCPUs. That being said, I was originally concerned with reducing any sort of latency since the get/set_freq calls are in the hot path for the scheduler. However, there were no measurable performance differences when running through the benchmarks, so I ended up getting rid of the extra cpu policy data to simplify the driver a bit.",
      "parentUuid": "c5d84143_502db3a5",
      "revId": "ba35458c874bc0349c36b805a10200648e4b6738",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
